---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Timeline from "@/components/Timeline.astro";
const dev = import.meta.env.DEV;

const path = [
  {
    when: "Fall 2024 · ASEN 2012",
    title: "First simulations: ODE models with uncertainty",
    body: "Water-rocket thrust phases, and closest approach from noisy tracks.",
    href: "/projects/bottle-rocket/",
  },
  {
    when: "Spring 2026 · APPM 3050",
    title: "Finite differences for PDEs",
    body: "2-D wave and heat equations, and a tsunami over variable bathymetry with ghost-node boundaries.",
    href: "/projects/tsunami-simulation/",
  },
  {
    when: "Spring 2026 · ASEN 3802",
    title: "Panel methods and lifting-line theory",
    body: "First discretised aerodynamics: where inviscid theory works and where it stops.",
    href: "/coursework/#computational-aero",
  },
  {
    when: "Aug 2026 · Personal",
    title: "Hypersonic boundary-layer and transition solver",
    body: "Compressible similarity BL, local-similarity marching, a corrected energy equation and an honest benchmark.",
    href: "/projects/hypersonic-solver/",
  },
  {
    when: "Fall 2026 · ASEN 5131",
    title: "Graduate hypersonic aerodynamics",
    body: "Entry trajectories and stagnation heating, then Newtonian aerodynamics of the Apollo capsule.",
    href: "/coursework/#hypersonics",
  },
  {
    when: "Fall 2027 · Goal",
    title: "Graduate research in CFD for hypersonic flows",
    body: "From engineering correlations to resolved simulations of transition and heating.",
    todo: true,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research interests"
  description="Khalil Sidani's research interests: CFD for hypersonic boundary layers, roughness-induced transition and aerothermodynamics, and verification of simulation codes."
>
  <!-- DRAFT: Research Interests copy. TODO(Lilo): rewrite in your own voice; add per-school notes below. -->
  <article class="wrap pt-12 md:pt-16">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research interests {dev && <span class="draft-badge ml-2">draft</span>}</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-[clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3rem)]">
        Predicting heating and transition on hypersonic vehicles, and knowing how far to trust the prediction
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-muted">
        I want to work on computational methods for hypersonic boundary layers: laminar–turbulent transition, surface
        heating and the aerothermodynamics that set thermal-protection design. In the long run I want to build
        commercial high-speed aircraft, and that makes these the questions I most need answered well.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_20rem]">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Questions that interest me</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>How far do engineering transition correlations transfer?</strong> In my solver, the choice of
            roughness height alone moved the predicted onset by more than 4° and scattered three correlations that
            otherwise agreed. I want to understand, with resolved simulations, which physical length scale really
            controls roughness-induced transition on blunt bodies.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>When does local similarity stop being good enough?</strong> Marching downstream exposed a missing
            pressure-work term and the limits of similarity solutions at finite edge Mach number. The next step is
            non-similar and full Navier–Stokes boundary layers, and knowing in advance where each level of model fails.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>How much does thermochemical nonequilibrium change stagnation heating?</strong> My solver assumes a
            calorically perfect gas. Two-temperature models change the wall heat flux at high enthalpy, and I want to
            learn to model that properly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>How do we verify the codes we rely on?</strong> Exact identities (I used Crocco–Busemann and total
            enthalpy), manufactured solutions, cross-code comparisons and honest uncertainty. Every project on this site
            is built around one of these.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>What I bring</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Verification as a habit.</strong> Python against NASA CARA to 8.9 × 10⁻⁸, a simulator against an
            analytic model to 0.09 %, a solver against Fay–Riddell to −1.45 %.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Honest reporting.</strong> A machine-learning model that tied its baseline is reported as a tie, and
            a benchmark gap I couldn't close is shown with its diagnosis.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Software practice.</strong> Tested code (144 pytest tests on the solver), equations traced to their
            sources, and decisions recorded with their reasons.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Breadth to connect pieces.</strong> Flight dynamics, orbital mechanics, aircraft sizing and
            experiment, not only one code.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What I want to learn next</h2>
        <p>
          Finite-volume methods for compressible flow with shock capturing, high-order schemes for boundary-layer
          stability, and working inside an established research code. I'd rather learn these from a group doing it than
          on my own.
        </p>

        <h2 id="schools">Program and lab notes</h2>
        <div class="not-prose rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-dashed border-line-strong p-5 text-sm text-muted">
          <p class="mono-label text-warn">To be filled in</p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Space for notes on specific programs, labs and advisors: why each group fits, which of its papers I've read,
            and how my work connects. <!-- TODO(Lilo): add per-school entries, or remove this section. -->
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside aria-labelledby="path-h">
        <h2 id="path-h" class="text-lg">
          How the projects build toward it
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-5">
          <Timeline items={path} />
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
